</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks and Datasets.</span> We evaluate ReSSFormer on three task categories: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Long-context reasoning</span> with NarrativeQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kočiskỳ et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>; (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scalable modeling efficiency</span> via Wikitext-103 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and PG-19 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2020</a>)</cite>; and (3) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Structure generalization</span> using TabFact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, OGB-Arxiv <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and a synthetic shuffled-paragraph QA benchmark. For all datasets, we follow standard preprocessing, train-validation-test splits, and evaluation metrics (e.g., exact match, F1, accuracy, node classification AUC).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare ReSSFormer against Transformer variants: GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, Longformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beltagy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, BigBird <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zaheer et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and Performer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choromanski et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. We also include structured baselines such as Graphformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ying et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and TabTransformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span> All models are trained using AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017</a>)</cite> with linear warmup and cosine decay. For ReSSFormer, we use <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=4</annotation></semantics></math> recurrent iterations and top-<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=32</annotation></semantics></math> attention sparsity unless otherwise stated. Memory size <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>, with attention pooling using learned score-weighted average. MoE routing activates <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=2</annotation></semantics></math> out of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=8</annotation></semantics></math> experts per head. We implement all components in PyTorch atop HuggingFace Transformers and we adopt early stopping based on dev accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Long-Context Reasoning Benchmarking</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark ReSSFormer’s long-context reasoning which require integrating information across multi-thousand-token inputs. Context lengths range from 1k to 8k tokens. Models are trained on 4k tokens and tested with longer inputs. We compare ReSSFormer against GPT-2, Longformer, BigBird, and RoPE-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, all with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>125M parameters and similar FLOPs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2. Long-Context Reasoning Benchmarking ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ ReSSFormer: A Recursive Sparse Structured Transformer for Scalable and Long-Context Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, ReSSFormer sustains performance up to 8k tokens, while others degrade beyond 2k. This stems from its R2MU-based evidence accumulation and ASAM’s robustness to irrelevant context. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2. Long-Context Reasoning Benchmarking ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ ReSSFormer: A Recursive Sparse Structured Transformer for Scalable and Long-Context Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows ReSSFormer also achieves higher accuracy at similar runtime, indicating efficient scaling without added complexity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01585v1/long_context_curve.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/238;" width="452" height="238"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Accuracy vs. input length.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
